<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osun State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osun State is a state in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, created in 1991 from part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> State, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a>. It covers much of the old Ijesha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> lands, including Ilesa and Ile-Ife, regarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> as their ancestral home. It takes its name from the Osun river and goddess, whose sacred grove at Osogbo is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osun-state.jpg" alt="Osun State" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: S.aderogba · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erin-ijesha-water-fall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Osun State lies in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, bounded by Ekiti and Ondo states to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwara-state">Kwara State</a> to the north, Ogun State to the south and Oyo State to the west, mostly across the Osun River. It takes its name from the river, which bisects the state's interior before forming much of its south-western border, and the Erinle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> are tributaries. Most of the state lies in the Nigerian lowland forests, with the drier Guinean forest–savanna mosaic in the north. The people are primarily <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, of the Ibolo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbomina">Igbomina</a>, Ijesha and Oyo subgroups, and the climate is tropical wet and dry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the pre-colonial period the area was divided among various western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> states, some based on a single town and some part of larger empires such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>. From 1877 to 1893 the Kiriji War was fought among Yoruba states and ended in a stalemate brokered by the British, after which the area was colonised and incorporated into the British Southern Nigeria Protectorate, merged into British Nigeria in 1914. After independence in 1960 it belonged to the Western Region, and from 1967 to the Western State. In 1976 the west of the Western State became Oyo State, and on 27 August 1991 Osun State was formed from the south-east of Oyo State, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a>.</p><p>The name comes from the River Osun, the venerated spring that is the manifestation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> goddess of the same name. Every August adherents and others travel from many countries to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-osogbo">Osun-Osogbo</a> festival, among them visitors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, and the Osun-Osogbo Grove, the shrine of the annual rites and an important artistic centre, was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2005.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and English are the official languages, and the people practise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the traditional faith. The economy rests largely on agriculture, with cocoa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, cashew, banana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> and yam, along with services, artisanal mining and livestock. Obafemi Awolowo University stands in the ancient town of Ile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>, an important early centre of Yoruba political and religious development. The state also holds many ancient kingdom capitals, among them Ila Orangun, Iragbiji, Ikirun, Ede, Iwo and Ilesa, and festivals such as Olojo in Ife and Iwude Ijesha in Ilesa. Erin-Ijesha Waterfalls, with seven levels, is a tourist attraction.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osun State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today Ife is a city of more than 500,000 people, the largest in <b>Osun State</b> by the 2006 census, divided between the Ife East and Ife Central local government areas; the Ooni is prominent among Nigeria's royal obas, and the festivals of the orisha continue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It then came under British control within Southern Nigeria, and the Owa Obokun still reigns at Ilesa in <b>Osun State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ijesha-kingdom">Ijesha Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
